//// 1.0.10-beta `00 · 04-js` D6 — a function that calls ITSELF in tail position
//// must not grow the stack. Measured by rakun's front 05: `std`'s
//// `random.intInRange` walks one frame per unit of range (its `floorWalk`
//// helper is hand-written tail recursion), so a range of a few thousand killed
//// the program on node and nowhere else — erlang and beam drop the frame, node
//// does not, and commonJS emitted a plain JS call.
////
//// The bound is a VARIABLE on purpose: a literal one could be folded and hide
//// the depth. 20 000 is above the ceiling the old lowering had and below the
//// one wasm still has, measured at `3b1e2468` + this front's fix:
////
////   commonJS  the old `return sumDown(n - 1, …)` shape: 10 000 ok, 20 000
////             `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`
////   wasm      still recursion (`codegen/wat.zig` belongs to another front):
////             30 000 ok, 40 000 `wasm trap: call stack exhausted`
////   erlang    no ceiling that matters — a tail-recursive VM
////
//// `depth` is the other half of the rule and is deliberately shallow: the
//// recursion there is under an operator, so it is NOT a tail call and still
//// uses the stack on every backend. That is the documented limit, not an
//// oversight — see `docs.md` § Recursion.

fn sumDown(n: i32, acc: i32) -> i32 {
    if (n == 0) return acc;
    return sumDown(n - 1, acc + n);
}

fn depth(n: i32) -> i32 {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    return 1 + depth(n - 1);
}

pub fn main() {
    val bound = 20000;
    @print(sumDown(bound, 0));
    @print(depth(100));
}
